    "question": {
      ":question/id": "08983fa5-3ea7-52cb-8387-e78357c6c71b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Rewrite $\\frac{225}{4}-15y+y^2$ in the form $(y+a)^2+b$. Find the signed numbers $a$ and $b$.\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$b=$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put the quadratic term first: $\\frac{225}{4}-15y+y^2=y^2-15y+\\frac{225}{4}$.\n\nThe half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^2-15y+\\frac{225}{4}&=\\underbrace{y^2-2\\cdot \\frac{15}{2}y+\\frac{225}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{225}{4}+\\frac{225}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(y-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{225}{4}+\\frac{225}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(y-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-15y$.\n\nComparing with $(y+a)^2+b$ gives $a=-\\frac{15}{2}$ and $b=0$.",
